<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lázaro Cárdenas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lázaro Cárdenas del Río</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lázaro Cárdenas was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from 1934 to 1940. A general of the Revolution, he carried out the largest land reform in Mexican history, distributing millions of hectares as ejidos, and supported organised labour. In 1938 he expropriated the foreign-owned oil industry and created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pemex">Pemex</a>. He also reorganised the ruling party into the forerunner of the PRI and gave refuge to Spanish Republicans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas.jpg" alt="Lázaro Cárdenas" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A1zaro.C%C3%A1rdenas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lázaro Cárdenas was born on 21 May 1895 in Jiquilpan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, one of eight children in a lower-middle-class family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> ancestry; his father owned a billiard hall. After his father's death in 1911 he supported his mother and seven younger siblings, working as a tax collector, a printer's devil and a jail keeper. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> overthrew Francisco Madero in 1913, serving under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancho-villa">Pancho Villa</a>, and after 1915 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarco-elias-calles">Plutarco Elías Calles</a>. He became a brigadier general at 25 and later governor of Michoacán. Calles chose him as presidential candidate in 1934, and nobody expected him to be anything but loyal to Calles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cárdenas was inaugurated on 1 December 1934, cut his salary in half and turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-castle">Chapultepec Castle</a> into the National Museum of History. Calles expected to keep ruling behind the presidency, but Cárdenas out-maneuvered him and forced him into exile. He restructured the ruling party, renamed the Party of the Mexican Revolution in 1938, on the sectoral representation of peasants, labour unions and the army; bringing the army in was meant to reduce its power to stage coups. Using Article 27 of the constitution, he redistributed some 180,000 square kilometres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> land to peasants and created ejidos, with major expropriations in La Laguna and Yucatán. He promoted the Confederation of Mexican Workers, and created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Indigenous Affairs in 1936.</p><p>In 1936 the oil workers' union, with 18,000 members, forced the foreign-owned oil companies to sign a first collective bargaining agreement. When the companies refused to pay the wages that the Council of Conciliation and Arbitration and, on 1 March 1938, the Supreme Court upheld, Cárdenas nationalized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s petroleum reserves and expropriated the companies' equipment on 18 March 1938. He also supported the Spanish Republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and offered exile to as many as 20,000-40,000 Spanish refugees; the Casa de España became the Colegio de México in October 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cárdenas endorsed the moderate Manuel Ávila Camacho as his successor and gave up power in December 1940, an achievement later writers stress. He was the first post-revolutionary president to serve a full six-year term. Commentators have called him the most constructive radical of the Revolution and also an authoritarian populist. He later served as Secretary of National Defence and died on 19 October 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lázaro Cárdenas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolution produced Article 27 of the Constitution of 1917, which made subsoil resources national property and allowed land redistribution; under <b>Lázaro Cárdenas</b> in the 1930s some 18 million hectares were distributed, largely as communal ejidos, and the foreign oil companies were expropriated in 1938 to form Pemex.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jiquilpan de Juárez, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.99,-102.72&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Jiquilpan de Juárez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1779 1423 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lázaro Cárdenas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2229 1693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2229 1693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.99,-102.72&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
